diff --git a/cloudflare_worker/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-25-web-personal-direct-messages-design.md b/cloudflare_worker/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-25-web-personal-direct-messages-design.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..617f09359
--- /dev/null
+++ b/cloudflare_worker/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-25-web-personal-direct-messages-design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,181 @@
+# Web Personal Direct Messages Design
+
+## Summary
+
+ForkMesh `/chat` will add Slack-style one-to-one direct messages for active registered users.
+Signed-in users can start a conversation from a Direct messages picker or from a Message action beside a registered user in the People pane.
+The same pair of users always reopens one shared conversation.
+Only the two participants may discover the conversation, obtain its room key, or connect to its WebSocket.
+
+Direct messages continue to use ForkMesh's AES-256-GCM authenticated shared-key transport and seven-day bounded retained history.
+They are not end-to-end encrypted against the relay because the relay derives and distributes the room passphrase.
+
+## Goals
+
+- Add a Direct messages section to the full `/chat` room sidebar.
+- Let an active registered user find another active registered user and start a one-to-one conversation.
+- Add a Message shortcut to registered-user rows in the People pane without removing the existing profile link.
+- Reuse an existing conversation when either participant starts the same pair again.
+- Make a newly started conversation appear automatically for both participants.
+- Preserve messages, attachments, reactions, edits, deletes, unread counts, and reconnect behavior inside DMs.
+- Keep guests, nodes, unrelated users, and platform administrators who are not participants from discovering or entering a DM.
+- Keep the existing public and private channel behavior unchanged.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Group direct messages are outside this change.
+- Self-messaging is outside this change.
+- Blocking, muting, message requests, and abuse-report workflows are outside this change.
+- Permanent message retention and full-text message search are outside this change.
+- Read receipts, typing indicators, push notifications, and email notifications are outside this change.
+- Desktop and mobile-client DM changes are outside this web `/chat` implementation.
+- Hiding message plaintext from the relay is outside this change because the existing web chat protocol uses relay-derived shared keys.
+
+## Considered Approaches
+
+### Dedicated direct-message conversations
+
+This approach stores one opaque conversation per normalized participant pair and stores both participants in a separate membership table.
+It gives DMs an authorization policy that never grants implicit administrator access while reusing the existing encrypted room transport.
+This is the selected approach because the security boundary is clear and independently testable.
+
+### Special private channels
+
+This approach would add a `direct` kind to `chat_channels` and represent both users as channel members.
+It would reuse more code, but current private channels grant all platform administrators implicit access and include channel-management behavior that DMs must never inherit.
+Preventing those rules from leaking into DMs would make the shared abstraction harder to reason about.
+
+### Client-only private room names
+
+This approach would derive a room name from the two usernames in the browser and skip a server-side conversation record.
+It is rejected because it would expose identity in routing, make authorization and discovery unreliable, and make duplicate or renamed accounts difficult to handle safely.
+
+## Storage Model
+
+Migration `0079` and the lazy schema add `chat_direct_conversations` and `chat_direct_participants`.
+
+`chat_direct_conversations` contains:
+
+- `conversation_id`, a random opaque 128-bit identifier used in APIs and room routing.
+- `pair_bi`, a keyed blind index of the two sorted account blind indexes that enforces one conversation per pair.
+- `data`, an encrypted object containing the two canonical public usernames.
+- `created_at` and `updated_at` timestamps.
+- `key_version`, a positive integer beginning at `1`.
+
+`chat_direct_participants` contains:
+
+- `conversation_id`, the referenced direct conversation.
+- `participant_bi`, one participant's existing account blind index.
+- `data`, an encrypted object containing that participant's canonical username.
+- `joined_at`, the conversation creation timestamp.
+- A composite primary key on `conversation_id` and `participant_bi`.
+- An index on `participant_bi` for efficient per-user conversation listing and authorization.
+
+Conversation creation resolves both active user accounts, rejects self-messaging, sorts their account blind indexes, and derives the pair blind index.
+The API returns the existing conversation if the pair already exists.
+Otherwise it writes the conversation and exactly two participant rows in one D1 batch.
+Human-readable usernames stay inside encrypted data.
+
+## Authorization Model
+
+Every DM endpoint requires an active registered-user session.
+Guests, nodes, disabled users, missing sessions, and malformed sessions receive `401 invalid_session` on the collection route.
+
+Only a participant may list a direct conversation, request its room access, or connect to its WebSocket.
+Platform administrator status does not grant implicit access.
+Unknown and unauthorized conversation resource routes return the same `404 not_found` response so they do not become existence or participant oracles.
+
+The room-access endpoint and WebSocket admission independently recheck current active-user status and participant membership.
+The short-lived WebSocket ticket binds the conversation ID, key version, participant account blind index, and expiry.
+Tickets, passphrases, and derived CryptoKeys remain in memory and are never written to browser storage.
+
+## API Contract
+
+### `GET /api/chat/direct-messages`
+
+Requires an active registered-user session.
+Returns only conversations where the requester has a participant row.
+Each item contains `id`, `otherUser`, `updatedAt`, and `keyVersion`.
+
+### `POST /api/chat/direct-messages`
+
+Requires an active registered-user session.
+Accepts `{ "username": "alice" }`.
+The target must be another active registered user.
+It returns the existing conversation with status `200`, or creates the pair atomically and returns it with status `201`.
+An unknown or inactive target returns `404 user_not_found`.
+The current user's own username returns `400 cannot_message_self`.
+
+### `GET /api/chat/direct-messages/{conversation_id}/room-access`
+
+Requires participant membership.
+Returns the conversation identity, current key version, relay-derived passphrase, and a signed WebSocket URL valid for 60 seconds.
+It uses `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+
+### `GET /api/chat/direct-messages/{conversation_id}/ws?ticket=...`
+
+Requires a valid short-lived ticket and WebSocket upgrade.
+The Worker rechecks the current key version, participant membership, and active account status before selecting the Durable Object.
+Invalid, expired, unrelated, disabled, and unknown access returns `404 not_found`.
+
+## Room Isolation and Retention
+
+The Worker derives a DM passphrase from `DATA_KEY`, the opaque conversation ID, and key version using a DM-specific derivation label.
+The Durable Object room name is `chat-direct:<conversation_id>:v<key_version>`.
+This isolates every DM from public World `#general`, administrator-created channels, repository rooms, and every other DM.
+
+DM frames use the existing encrypted chat envelope and durable `chat_history` retention path.
+The relay retains at most 500 frames and 16 MiB per room for seven days.
+Attachments retain the existing one MiB decoded-file limit.
+
+## Web Chat Experience
+
+The left pane keeps the existing Rooms section and adds a Direct messages section below it for signed-in users.
+The section header includes an accessible New direct message button.
+Existing DM rows show the other participant's avatar, username, active state, and unread badge.
+
+The new-message dialog uses the existing registered-user directory and provides a searchable list.
+It excludes the signed-in user and starts the selected conversation immediately.
+Repeated selection of the same user reopens the existing conversation without creating another record.
+
+Registered-user rows in the People pane keep their profile link and gain a separate Message action.
+The action is hidden for guests, nodes, the current user, and users without an active registered-user session.
+
+Selecting a DM closes the prior room connection, requests fresh room access, derives the DM-specific key, opens the ticketed WebSocket, and renders retained and live messages.
+The center header displays `@username`, the visibility badge displays `direct`, and the composer placeholder displays `Message @username...`.
+Channel administration controls remain hidden in DMs.
+
+The client polls the lightweight DM list alongside the existing channel list.
+A newly started conversation therefore appears for the other participant without a page reload.
+The selected conversation ID may be stored as an opaque local preference.
+The client falls back to `#general` if the selected DM is no longer authorized.
+
+The narrow layout keeps the existing horizontal conversation selector behavior and includes DM rows without horizontal overflow.
+
+## Error Handling
+
+An expired session clears authorized DM state, returns the user to `#general`, disables DM actions, and shows the existing sign-in guidance.
+An unavailable relay leaves the current UI readable, reports the existing relay-unavailable status, and retries through the normal polling and room-reconnect paths.
+Starting a DM reports `user_not_found`, `cannot_message_self`, or a generic request failure inline in the dialog without closing it.
+Room authorization failure releases stale in-memory room material and returns to `#general`.
+
+## Testing
+
+Backend unit tests cover schema parity, pair uniqueness, atomic creation, idempotent reverse-order creation, self-message rejection, session requirements, participant-only listing, room access, and administrator non-bypass.
+Ticket and socket tests cover invalid tickets, expired tickets, wrong key versions, unrelated users, disabled users, and exact Durable Object room naming.
+
+Frontend contract tests cover the Direct messages controls, separate profile and Message actions, opaque preference storage, dialog accessibility, DM-specific room access, and fallback behavior.
+Playwright tests reproduce the user experience with Alice starting a DM with Bob, sending encrypted text and an attachment, Bob discovering and reopening it, unread state, duplicate prevention, mobile layout, and unrelated administrator denial.
+
+## Acceptance Criteria
+
+- A signed-in user can start a one-to-one DM with another active registered user from `/chat`.
+- A signed-in user can start the same DM from either the new-message picker or the People pane.
+- Both participants see one shared conversation regardless of who starts it or how many times it is opened.
+- Only the two participants can list, authorize, connect to, and read the DM.
+- Platform administrators who are not participants have no implicit access.
+- Guests and nodes cannot discover or start DMs.
+- Text, reactions, edits, deletes, images, and documents use the existing encrypted retained protocol inside DMs.
+- The active DM has correct heading, composer, unread, reconnect, and fallback behavior.
+- The feature remains accessible and free of horizontal overflow on a 390-pixel viewport.
+- Existing public and private channel tests continue to pass unchanged.
